The scheduled per-session checkpoint tick is DISABLED as of 2026-08-11.

Why: measured on 2026-08-10 it fired 71 times and produced 9 useful digest updates. Each firing
was a separate API request that re-uploaded the whole session — roughly a third of that day's
~406 requests, on the order of 9M input tokens. The per-tick text was never the cost; the
context re-upload was. The premise was also wrong: idle time was assumed free, but idle is
exactly when the prompt cache has expired, so an idle tick is the most expensive kind.

Cron jobs are session-scoped and invisible to other sessions, so a tick armed yesterday cannot
be cancelled from here. This file makes `scripts/session-extract.sh` refuse and tells that
session to cancel its own job.

What replaces it: `scripts/session-snapshot.sh` — a detached loop that gates on a shell-only
count and involves no model at all, so an idle session costs nothing. Consolidation into the
digest happens rarely, in a separate cheap-model process. See `session/CONVENTIONS.md`
§ Checkpoint capture.

Delete this file only to deliberately re-enable the old mechanism.
